{:edb/db-before-t 373 :edb/db-after-t 374 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533686 50 #inst "2026-10-04T11:34:51.073Z" 13194139533686 true] [17592186175846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246825 13194139533686 true] [17592186175846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246828 13194139533686 true] [17592186175846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246832 13194139533686 true] [17592186175846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246851 13194139533686 true] [17592186175846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246858 13194139533686 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246808 13194139533686 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246815 13194139533686 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246835 13194139533686 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246865 13194139533686 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246872 13194139533686 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246801 13194139533686 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246818 13194139533686 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246838 13194139533686 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246841 13194139533686 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186246844 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246802 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1089 "q-103449" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1100 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1113 #uuid "a074bb7c-f733-505c-a777-082de91b8ef3" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246801 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246803 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246804 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246805 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246806 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246807 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1041 "selection" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246807 13194139533686 true] [17592186246802 1110 #uuid "9e38ef1a-4ee3-531d-9211-cd1bb4399c8d" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246803 1034 "3x-2y=-10" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246803 1111 #uuid "10d61639-c435-562a-97a3-b12c12a64b3c" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246804 1034 "x+2y=2" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246804 1111 #uuid "fd636abb-fd4a-5e3b-be7b-2a1101756255" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246805 1034 "3x+y=-4" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246805 1111 #uuid "2ae18454-d341-5cc7-a2fd-d2f7a037e52b" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246806 1034 "x-2y=-6" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246806 1111 #uuid "fb1e04fa-e872-5646-ad5d-17579f4684f1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246807 1034 "3x+2y=-2" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246807 1111 #uuid "850e5bba-4731-5599-b16e-7a8c7a7e037d" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246809 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1089 "q-103455" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1100 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(8,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3).$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1113 #uuid "be690892-d1ee-5b6d-ab92-cca99b162dcd" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246808 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(8,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{5}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{5}{4}x-10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{4}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246810 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246811 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246812 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246813 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246814 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1041 "selection" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246812 13194139533686 true] [17592186246809 1110 #uuid "e3747c76-2265-5b79-a09e-d2ce5fc7e463" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246810 1034 "y=-\\frac{5}{4}x+9" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246810 1111 #uuid "1980acbe-516b-5de3-9b3a-9f748634a857" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246811 1034 "y=\\frac{4}{5}x-9" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246811 1111 #uuid "072f2194-5ab6-5087-a400-4b663f6fdd1a" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246812 1034 "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246812 1111 #uuid "aa17e738-55de-5a4b-9fce-3a293aa00ee5" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246813 1034 "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+19" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246813 1111 #uuid "8a75a8ca-4c3a-5af5-8fd9-918b1d690275" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246814 1034 "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+9" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246814 1111 #uuid "88a7fdca-71da-547f-8e38-73ad5f0a19b6" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246816 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1089 "q-215768" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1100 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1113 #uuid "63ce0ff0-d49c-5492-b89a-ea6a5e332526" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246815 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-4 & =x \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246817 13194139533686 true] [17592186246816 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246816 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246816 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246817 13194139533686 true] [17592186246816 1110 #uuid "97e1f251-70da-5c6a-8984-d48773fc9069" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246817 1034 "x+4" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246817 1111 #uuid "e0735bfe-94a4-58e2-a10b-385d490997ec" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246819 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1089 "q-23631" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1100 "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1113 #uuid "ebba1590-9e65-500b-8c4b-a4d7ba7a650f" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246818 1145 "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246820 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246821 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246822 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246823 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246824 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1041 "selection" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246824 13194139533686 true] [17592186246819 1110 #uuid "d8569deb-6cf7-5d5c-84ef-2474c64086d3" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246820 1034 "x+y=0" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246820 1111 #uuid "b322489e-e53b-5226-af85-c6d93ba9eb20" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246821 1034 "2x-3y=-5" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246821 1111 #uuid "58c1935e-ff78-5933-b4e4-51a93992c003" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246822 1034 "3x-2y=-5" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246822 1111 #uuid "18ba6f8c-015d-54f0-9a37-e5112a5a46b7" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246823 1034 "2x+3y=1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246823 1111 #uuid "deca52ed-b51e-5fd8-b96b-ecd8190eb94f" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246824 1034 "3x+2y=-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246824 1111 #uuid "703bd43f-1439-51e0-8b47-3575c00f6846" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246826 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1089 "q-237031" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1100 "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to $y=4x-6$ that passes through the point $(8,-3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1113 #uuid "130d5595-7cbe-51db-aac8-83150d0860ae" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246825 1145 "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=4x-6,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(8,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246827 13194139533686 true] [17592186246826 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246826 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246826 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246827 13194139533686 true] [17592186246826 1110 #uuid "c46413f9-b558-53bd-9f22-11d016cba92f" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246827 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}x-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246827 1111 #uuid "fc55c0b3-1870-5d3e-acd7-aff99013c4d9" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246829 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1089 "q-237193" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/5add9f877dc2e93b1c8e562794aa64c83d180f5c683735055ee24f8a190e8740.png)\n\nThe equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=-4x-3$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$  What is the equation of ${l}_{2}?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1113 #uuid "7d7b950d-4468-5f50-9499-9d01afa18ed5" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246828 1145 "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $-4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-4) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246830 13194139533686 true] [17592186246829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246831 13194139533686 true] [17592186246829 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246829 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246829 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246830 13194139533686 true] [17592186246829 1110 #uuid "3a66b41c-db0d-5cca-912a-80b5be4da9fd" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246830 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}x+1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246830 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246830 1111 #uuid "1299bd78-18f8-50fb-8baf-2c768dace883" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246831 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}x+2" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246831 1111 #uuid "2cbc16bf-8e6b-5824-a7d5-7a0dec6c3cc0" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246833 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1089 "q-237197" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1100 "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. If the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(6,-1),$ what is the value of $b?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1113 #uuid "3e4defe2-68c2-5032-be33-7cb859509d28" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246832 1145 "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{2}{5},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(6,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-6) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+15 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=14.$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246834 13194139533686 true] [17592186246833 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246833 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246833 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246834 13194139533686 true] [17592186246833 1110 #uuid "0827810c-2459-54cc-bf9f-a6afaf30eccd" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246834 1034 "14" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246834 1111 #uuid "1813979a-422c-5365-beae-dc322fe363db" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246836 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1089 "q-237224" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1100 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1113 #uuid "d3e3189e-08d0-518b-8225-73f15324974f" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246835 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6}{5}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6}{5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246837 13194139533686 true] [17592186246836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246836 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246837 13194139533686 true] [17592186246836 1110 #uuid "23819259-60f2-55cc-98b9-8bbc4ba2b535" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246837 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246837 1111 #uuid "57488bd3-83d5-5db6-94fd-b8a126d276a7" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246839 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1089 "q-237230" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1100 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,3)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1113 #uuid "62a37dcc-7584-5c66-9685-db6471626299" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246838 1145 "First, we write the equation $5x+y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-5x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-5x+2.$ The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+4) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246840 13194139533686 true] [17592186246839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246839 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246840 13194139533686 true] [17592186246839 1110 #uuid "b6f1759f-0b67-5953-a229-7ecd8422ffd7" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246840 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246840 1111 #uuid "6a1e4813-3faa-51f3-a6c7-5f380a270cde" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246842 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1089 "q-237231" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1100 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,0)$ and is perpendicular to $x-6y=-3?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1113 #uuid "f783ab8b-742a-5e04-8610-0016de741818" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246841 1145 "First, we write the equation $x-6y=-3$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6y & =-3 \\\\ -6y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-6.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-6$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-6(x-(-7)) \\\\ y & =-6(x+7) \\\\ y & =-6x-42\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246843 13194139533686 true] [17592186246842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533686 true] [17592186246842 1041 "field-1" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246843 13194139533686 true] [17592186246842 1110 #uuid "f6bd0d56-e288-5c4a-9f2c-1d0c00ef8147" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246843 1034 "-6x-42" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246843 1111 #uuid "fdc38579-e69e-53f0-b1d2-d53f0ff0043c" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246845 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1089 "q-252524" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1100 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8).$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1113 #uuid "c19ac983-6286-56e7-bf4f-85d61c5577fb" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246844 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes, which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3) \\\\ 2(y-4) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-x+3 \\\\ 2y & =-x+11 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x+2y & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246846 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246847 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246848 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246849 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246850 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1041 "selection" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246847 13194139533686 true] [17592186246845 1110 #uuid "3b4e7f27-bcce-5fe9-a537-b648f9f75292" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246846 1034 "2x+5y=26" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246846 1111 #uuid "81266fc1-fe0c-51e1-9374-4ba7b1deca75" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246847 1034 "x+2y=11" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246847 1111 #uuid "a920e7b8-ecbd-5444-a4b7-a9bf8fdfc6ba" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246848 1034 "2x+y=10" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246848 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246848 1111 #uuid "605d03d7-51e9-53a3-887a-8482ecc9319e" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246849 1034 "x+4y=19" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246849 1111 #uuid "097d4e64-f405-57b6-bd5b-957c47f6e9d3" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246850 1034 "2x-y=2" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246850 1111 #uuid "ed224373-3e75-571a-8299-ea9867c56239" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246852 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1089 "q-44337" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1113 #uuid "a15bc33a-bff2-50cc-9ade-d155c462a5f8" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246851 1145 "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246853 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246854 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246855 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246856 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246857 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1041 "selection" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1104 #edb/ref 17592186246857 13194139533686 true] [17592186246852 1110 #uuid "c9da41e2-2923-5cf4-91a0-ca53e7a6f47f" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246853 1034 "y=2x+6" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246853 1111 #uuid "a46fa97f-4ae5-5696-9899-6c8f09cdf115" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246854 1034 "y=-2x+6" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246854 1111 #uuid "fe3a49f2-0382-59c8-a332-fd0e435b7bbb" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246855 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246855 1111 #uuid "8b8f924c-0567-5497-be06-5d4ff9eeb0dd" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246856 1034 "y=-6x-2" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246856 1111 #uuid "773f0b0b-9b58-5637-ab2e-a4f69d3b9435" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246857 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533686 true] [17592186246857 1111 #uuid "826b35cd-a0a2-5e29-bfc3-52e7878fb547" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1017 #edb/ref 17592186246859 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1089 "q-44606" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1100 "The lines $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. Given that the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(-1,5),$ what is the value of $b?$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1113 #uuid "8dccbd68-1234-5f4e-b21e-948f34cd05c0" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1138 false 13194139533686 true] [17592186246858 1145 "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-2$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x+1) \\\\ y-5 & =-2x-2 \\\\ y & =-2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=3.$" 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246860 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246861 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246862 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246863 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186246864 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533686 true] [17592186246859